scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1043:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof is on the defendant in an action for declaration of title to property where the legal title is in the plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the defendant.,"In an action for declaration of title to property, where the legal title is in the Plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the Defendant, the burden of proof is on the Defendant - See. Siyaneris vs Jayasinghe Udenis de Silva 52 NLR 289(PC) .",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1044:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_561_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_561_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"The trial judge must identify the land to be partitioned first, and if the parties are not in agreement about the identity of the corpus and boundaries, it is the duty of the court to identify the correct land.",If the parties are not in agreement about the identity of the corpus and boundaries it is the duty of court to identify the correct land.,SRC030-s44,section-not-found,section 44 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1051:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,"A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity, and a co-owner's possession in law is tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind unless there is proof of nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1053:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on a party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1056:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf,,COA,,"A plaintiff can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in uninterrupted possession of the land for ten years, without paying rent or performing any service or duty, and without acknowledging the rights of another person.",This position in law is supported by many authorities. Maasdorp's Institutes (7th Edition Vol2 at 96) Wigneswaran J in Leisa and another V Simon and Another [2002 (1) SLR 148] Pathirana V Jayasundera [58 NLR 169] Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera and others [1987 (1) SLR 235] Wijesundara and others v Constantine Dasa and another [1987 (2) SLR 66] I will now turn to consider whether the learned District Judge correctly evaluated the evidence and applied the law relevant thereto when he accepted the prescriptive rights .I 3 of the plaintiff rejecting the defence taken up by the defendant.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1057:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat ownership must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"lt is trite law that where a party to an action for a declaration of title to a land invokes the Provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1060:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A prescriptive title can be claimed if the respondent has been in possession of the land for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1061:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kalutara_534_95.pdf,,COA,,A certificate of sale does not divest the judgment-debtor's title to immovable property until the sale is confirmed by the court and a Fiscal's conveyance is executed.,The right and title of the judgment-debtor or of any person holding under him or deriving title through him to immovable property sold by virtue of an execution is not divested by the sale until the confirmation of the sale by the court and the execution of the Fiscal's conveyance.,SRC030-s289,section-not-found,section 289 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1064:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf,,COA,,A claim for recovery of property belonging to a temple is not barred by prescription under the Prescription Ordinance.,The Respondent's plea of prescription should necessarily fail.,SRC071-s34,ok-unchecked,section 34 of the Prescription Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1069:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf,,COA,,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated.,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated;,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1070:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_15_03.pdf,,COA,,"In effecting a partition, proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.",It is trite Law that in effecting a partition proper rights of way should be provided within the corpus to the district allotments as means of access to a public right of way.,SRC030-s754(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1071:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_la_420_2003.pdf,,COA,2003,A court may grant an extension of time to file objections under section 91A(1) and (2) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"Where a day is fixed or time appointed for doing any act or taking any proceeding by a party to the action, the court may, from time to time, upon the motions of such party and if sufficient cause is shown, fix another day or enlarge or abridge the time appointed, upon such terms, if any, as to it may seem proper.",SRC030-s91A,section-not-found,section 91A of the Civil Procedure Code,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1086:draftly-rule-courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf-1,courts-coa-RII-39-23-Final-Print.pdf,,COA,,A person affected by void proceedings can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court.,"The proceedings being void, the person affected by them can apply to have them set aside ex debito justitiae in the exercise of the inherent jurisdiction of the court which is saved by S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code.",SRC030-s839,section-not-found,S. 839 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1094:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc-_-appeal-_-39-_-2022_amd2.pdf,,SC,2022,"A power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, but this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized if the agent has not been objected to.","a power of attorney must be strictly construed as giving only such authority as it expressly or by necessary implication creates, this Court cannot decline to recognize the authority that the 1st Plaintiff himself has impliedly recognized what Nadarajah Thamilalagan had to do whatever was necessary or ordinarily incidental to the effective execution of an express authority conferred on him by a written document that got onto the record long after the appeal had been preferred to the Civil Appellate High Court of Jaffna.",SRC030-s17,section-not-found,Section 17 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1097:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action for damages arising from the delict of setting the law in motion is prescribed within 2 years of the arrest.,"Did the Court of Appeal fall into substantial error by completely ignoring the provisions of Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance? Moreover, in terms of section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance, action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.",SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1099:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of his co-owners.,A co-owner’s possession is in law the possession of his co-owners. It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind. Nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster could bring about the result.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1101:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"If the court is satisfied that there are grounds to object to an application, court should frame issues as in Section 533 of the Code and proceed with the matter.",In the manner issues are framed District Court need to answer same and ensure Justice is done.,SRC030-s533,section-not-found,Section 533 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1104:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_106_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A rightful owner of a property who was dispossessed by illegal means has the right to obtain an order from court for eviction and restoration.,A rightful owner of a property who was dispossessed by illegal means has the right to obtain an order from court for eviction and restoration.,SRC030-s839,section-not-found,section 839 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1110:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_110_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A settlement must be notified to the court by motion made in the presence of, or on notice to, all the parties concerned, and the court shall pass a decree in accordance therewith, so far as it relates to the action.","such agreement, compromise, or satisfaction shall be notified to the court by motion made in presence of, or on notice to, all the parties concerned, and the court shall pass a decree in accordance therewith, so far as it relates to the action, and such decree shall be final, so far as relates to so much of the subject-matter of the action as is dealt with by the agreement, compromise, or satisfaction.",SRC030-s408,section-not-found,Section 408 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1112:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_113_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"The High Court cannot set aside an arbitral award on grounds not specified in the Arbitration Act, and must make a finding based on the application and material contained therein.",A finding cannot be made by the High Court in terms of sub paragraph (b) of section 32 (1) other than on the averments of the application and the material contained therein.,SRC030-s32,section-not-found,section 32 (1) of the Arbitration Act No. 11 of 1995,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1116:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_116_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,The plaintiff must identify the land in suit with precision before the execution of the decree.,"If the plaintiff fails to identify the land he claims dominium over, his action must fail.",SRC030-s323,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1119:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_119_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A plaintiff cannot claim a bigger land than he is entitled to under and in terms of his chain of title.,"Therefore, the plaintiff was certainly not claiming a bigger land than he was entitled to under and in terms of his chain of title.",SRC030-s41,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1120:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_11_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,In a case where the party claiming a declaration of title must prove title to the corpus in dispute having identified the corpus in the first instance.,"It is trite law substantiated by a plethora of authorities, that in a case where the party claiming a declaration of title must prove title to the corpus in dispute having identified the corpus in the first instance.",SRC030-s432(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1121:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A contract by a minor is only voidable and not void, thus, the Power of Attorney remained valid until it is set aside and that subsequent setting aside of the Power of Attorney, will not affect the property rights acquired by the Petitioner as innocent third parties through the Power of Attorney.","Even if an unassisted contract is void against the minor it can be ratified and make valid by the minor after becoming a major as happened in the case at hand. Once it is ratified and made valid and used for his benefit, he is estopped from stating that it is void ab initio.",SRC071-s10,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1128:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_124_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A co-owner has an interest in every part of the entire land, and can eject a trespasser.","Thus, when a trespasser enjoys the fruits of the property the co-owner's rights are affected and he becomes entitled to eject the trespasser.",SRC030-s150,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1133:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A prosecution under the Food Act is time barred if it is instituted more than three months after the date of detection of the offence.,"It is contended that the intention of the Legislature by imposing a time bar as contained in Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act for instituting an action was to ensure inter alia credibility of the Complainant, as it is with the expectation that a Complainant will initiate an action by bringing the offence to the notice of the relevant Officers at the earliest point of discovery that the offence had taken place.",SRC071-s9,none,Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1136:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath for the period of prescription is insufficient to establish a servitude of right of way by prescription as per section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If prescription is allowed to be established merely by walking through someone’s land, it would pave the way for countless claims to be levelled against owners of land. It would give rise to drastic consequences in village settings where people casually walk through the lands of each other as a practice. If all persons casually exercising physical use of footpaths across neighboring lands were to bring their claims stating that each such footpath constitutes a right of way by prescription simply by physical use, the doctrine of prescription would have far reaching and drastic consequences which were never intended by its proponents.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1138:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred is considered adverse possession.,"The words in section 3—“A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred”—have been held by this Court to contain not an illustration, but a definition of “adverse possession” (see Daniel v. Markar Ram. 1843-55, 2, Vand. Rep. 44, Carrim v. Dholl 2 CLR 12).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1150:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_142_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A substituted party in an action is estopped from taking a contrary or inconsistent position to that of the original party to the action.,"When the original party agreed to certain arrangements before the Court, the substituted party is estopped and bound by the said arrangements.",SRC030-s395,section-not-found,Section 395 of the Civil Procedure Code Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1152:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A defendant cannot prove prescriptive title against co-owners unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, and possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1159:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to a co-owned property by proving exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others, if the possession is not hostile to the real owner and does not amount to a denial of the owner's title.",In order to acquire prescriptive title a co-owner should prove exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1165:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1171:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights and provide documentary evidence of their adverse possession.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,none,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1173:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A discretion under S-770 of the Civil Procedure Code is a very wide one and should be exercised cautiously.,On the other hand it is needless to stress that the discretion given under S - 770 is a very wide one and same has to be exercised cautiously which being a power expressly and plainly conferred on the Judge who hears the appeal.,SRC030-s770,section-not-found,Section 770 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1175:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.,"In our law, a co-owner can file an action against a trespasser or imminent trespasser to protect his rights and a Court can grant a lesser relief even where a bigger relief has been prayed for.",SRC030-s754(1),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1177:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_161_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_161_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A cause of action must accrue to a party before they can be made a plaintiff.,Hence no cause of action has accrued to the 3rd defendant against the 1st and 2nd defendants.,SRC030-s11,ok,section 11 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1183:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_166_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"A new point which was not raised in the issues or in the course of the trial cannot be raised for the 1st time in appeal, unless such point might have been raised at the trial under one of the issues raised, and the Court of Appeal has before it all the requisite material for deciding the point or the question is one of law and nothing more.","In the case of Setha Vs. Weerakoon 49 NLR 225, it was held, “A new point which was not raised in the issues or in the course of the trial cannot be raised for the 1st time in appeal, unless such point might have been raised at the trial under one of the issues raised, and the Court of Appeal has before it all the requisite material for deciding the point or the question is one of law and nothing more.”",SRC030-s545,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1188:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",I therefore hold that the Court of Appeal has erred when it held that the Plaintiff in order to institute this action should prove that the Plaintiff herself had lawful title.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1191:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A written promise to pay a sum of money must be in writing, but no special form of writing is required, and the promise may be contained in one document or evidenced by more than one document or by an exchange of documents.","For the purpose of constituting a written promise, contract, bargain or agreement no special form of writing is required.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1198:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_176_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A person can possess a property without occupying it, and to prove possession, it is not necessary to prove that he or she lives in the property or occupies it.",One can possess a property without occupying the same. To prove possession it is not necessary to prove that he or she lives in the property or occupies the property.,SRC030-s328,ok,Section 328 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1199:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_17_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_17_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A landlord need not establish any grounds of ejectment stipulated in section 22 of the Rent Act No. 7 of 1972 for success in his suit for ejectment if the tenant has repudiated the tenancy.,In such a case the landlord need not establish any one or more of the grounds of ejectment stipulated in section 22 of the Rent Act No. 7 of 1972 for success in his suit for ejectment.,SRC028-s22,ok-unchecked,section 22 of the Rent Act No. 7 of 1972,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1215:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A person who possesses a land with a secret intention that he would be able to claim prescription at the end of ten years is not entitled to claim prescription under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If a person, who claims prescription in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, possesses the property with a secret intention his possession cannot be considered as an adverse possession and as such he is not entitled to succeed in a claim of prescription.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1216:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach when determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code.,"In determining an application for the addition of parties under section 18(1) of the Civil Procedure Code, a wider and purposive construction must be preferred to a narrow and restrictive approach.",SRC030-s18,section-not-found,section 18 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1218:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A wife cannot claim prescriptive title to a property owned by her husband, as her possession of the property is not considered adverse possession.",Further when wife possesses a property of her husband she cannot claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance against her husband because such a possession cannot be considered to be an adverse possession against her husband.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1222:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,"A party who did not file the list of witnesses and documents as required by section 79B can invoke section 142D to call additional witnesses and produce additional documents at the pre-trial conference, but only if the other party consents to it.",A party who did not file the list as required by section 79B cannot invoke this provision unless the other party consents to it at the pre-trial conference.,SRC030-s79B,ok,section 79B of the Civil Procedure Code,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1226:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_212_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A judgment of a court of competent jurisdiction operates as an estoppel under the principle of estoppel per rem judicatam.,"where a final judicial decision has been pronounced by either an English, (or with certain exemptions) a foreign judicial tribunal of competent jurisdiction over the parties to, and the subject matter of, the litigation, any party or privy to such litigation, as against any other party or privy thereto, and, in the case of a decision in rem, any person whatsoever, as against any other person, is estopped in any subsequent litigation from disputing or questioning such decision on the merits, whether it be used as the foundation of an action, or relied upon as a bar to a claim, indictment or complaint, or to any affirmative defence, case, or allegation, if, but not unless, the party interested raises the point of estoppel at the proper time and in the proper manner.",SRC030-s207,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1240:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person who enters into occupation of property as a licensee cannot acquire prescriptive right to the property unless they produce an event or overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,"A person, who is let into occupation of property as a tenant, or as a licensee, must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act by which he manifests his intention of occupying it in another capacity.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1249:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,The possession of a property by a defendant is considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for more than ten years.,"In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1251:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.,"As explained above, time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1264:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period.,"In the case of Selamma Achie Vs Palavasam 41 NLR 186, the Supreme Court held that “ A court has no power to enter an order of abatement under Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code where the failure to prosecute the action for twelve months after the last order was due to the death of the plaintiff within that period”.",SRC030-s402,section-not-found,Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1271:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,"A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription and therefore, when the appellant was successful in case 17819/L, by Judgment dated 12.06.2009, the plea of prescriptive title by the respondent fail, as during the pendency of the successful action, the respondents have failed to be in uninterrupted and undisturbed possession for 10 years.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1274:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"In a rei vindicatio action, the burden of proof is on the defendant to show that he is in lawful possession, once the plaintiff's legal title to the corpus is proved.","Where the legal title to the corpus is proved, the burden of proof is on the Respondent to show that he is in lawful possession [Theivandran v. Ramanathan Chettiar (1986) 2 Sri LR 219 at 222; Wijetunge v. Thangarajah (1999) 1 Sri LR 53 at 55; Candappa Nee Bastian v. Ponnambalampillai (1993) 1 Sri LR 184 at 187; Beebi Johara v. Warusavithana (1998) 3 Sri LR 227 at 228].",SRC030-s772,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1276:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_41_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A trial judge must consider and answer the issues raised by the parties in a partition case, and must not fail to do so under the guise of not considering documents that were not tendered with the written submissions.","It is the very basic point in writing a judgment. It is so important that it is the accepted procedure that when issues are raised, the pleadings go to the background and the case is heard based on the points of contest meaning the issues raised by parties after putting down the admissions.",SRC030-s187,section-not-found,Section 187 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1278:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_42_2015_46_2015-1.pdf,,SC,2015,"The High Court should not summarily reject an application for enforcement of an arbitral award without giving an opportunity to tender duly certified copies, even if the copy of the award filed with the application is not a duly certified copy.",The claimant should have been given an opportunity to tender duly certified copies interpreting “accompany” in section 31 (2) purposively and widely (as in Sri Lanka General Workers’ Union v Samaranayake and Nagappa Chettiar v. Commissioner of Income Tax.),SRC030-s31(2),bad-source,section 31 (2) of the Arbitration Act,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1284:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A sale of immovable property cannot be set aside on the ground of irregularity unless the applicant proves to the satisfaction of the court that he has sustained substantial injury by reason of such irregularity, and unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal's report.",no sale shall be set aside on the ground of irregularity.....unless the grounds of the irregularity shall have been notified to the Court within thirty days of the receipt of the Fiscal’s Report,SRC030-s282,section-not-found,Section 282 (2) of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1288:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2003-part_II.pdf,,SC,2003,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,A trial Judge should assess the oral evidence and bring his mind to bear on the facts relevant to the dispute and give reasons for his decision of the dispute as required by Section 187 of the Code.,SRC030-s187,section-not-found,Section 187 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1292:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of a debt made after the limitation period has run is valid and effective to start time running again.,"In view of my finding that the Prescription Ordinance merely bars the remedy with lapse of time and does not extinguish the debt, there is no rational justification to insist that an acknowledgment must be made before the expiry of the limitation period to be effective. I hold that an acknowledgment of the debt to be effective for the purposes of section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance need not be made before the expiry of the period of limitation.",SRC071-s12,ok-unchecked,section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1293:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The District Court cannot permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,The District Court could not permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1301:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1304:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to a property if they have not demonstrated an intention to possess the property adversely to and independent of the owner's title, and have not manifested that intention to the owner.","To again apply the words of Bertram CJ in in TILLEKERATNE vs. BASTIAN, the defendant has not demonstrated to the plaintiff‟s father that the defendant had “an intention on his [her] part to possess adversely” and had not demonstrated “a manifestation of that intention” to the plaintiff‟s father.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1312:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim benefit of the law of the land.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1318:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming prescription must prove that there was undisturbed adverse possession for 10 years.,"Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance provides, “ Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1328:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,A party claiming prescriptive title must demonstrate a clear separation of the land in question from the remainder of the property.,"There was no cogent evidence led to demonstrate a clear separation of Lot B, on the northern boundary, from the remainder of the land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1335:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_71_72_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A party to a partition action who has been brought into substitute a deceased original defendant, who has failed and neglected to file a statement of claim or even a proxy despite notice/summons being served on such defendant, cannot be permitted to file fresh pleadings nearly 24 years after the institution of the action when the said action has reached the stage envisaged in section 184 of the Civil Procedure Code.",I answer that question in the negative.,SRC030-s184,section-not-found,section 184 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1336:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years to acquire a right of way by prescription.,It is a prerequisite to the acquisition of a right of way by prescription that a well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1339:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1340:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to a portion of a road if the final decree in a partition action has already wiped out all such rights.,"Nor can they claim any prescriptive rights to that portion because the Final Decree P4 wiped out all such rights, if they had any.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1346:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A party can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it as a separate land for several decades, independent of the title of the original owners.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1350:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,"For the defendant to prove 'adverse' possession, there should have been an intention by him to claim title to the land against its owner.","There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 (as amended),True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1354:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A subsequent owner who buys over the head of a tenant can get the benefit of section 2(4)(c) of the Rent Act No 55 of 1980, even if they did not occupy the premises on 1st January 1980, as long as the original owner was in occupation of the premises on that date and let the premises to the tenant after that date.",Therefore it is abundantly clear that the plaintiff is not estopped from having recourse to the exceptions as laid down in section 2(4)(c) of the Amendment to the Rent Act No 55 of 1980.,SRC028-s4,ok-unchecked,section 2(4)(c) of the Rent Act No 55 of 1980,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1358:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action can be filed if the plaintiff is disturbed but not ousted from the land in their occupation.,Disturbance of possession was protected by the mandament van maintenue. The applicant for such a relief had to give a concise account of his possession and of the disturbance caused by the other party.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1361:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_87_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"In a rei vindicatio action, the plaintiff must establish that he is the owner of the property.",In the case of D.A.Wanigaratne Vs Juwanis Appuhamy 65 NLR 167 wherein this court held that in action rei vindication the plaintiff must prove and establish his title.,SRC030-s146(2),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1362:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_88_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A party cannot put forward a ground of appeal for the first time in appeal unless it might have been put forward in the Court below and the matter in question should be a pure question of law.,"Accordingly the Appellate Court could consider a point raised for the first time in appeal, if the following requirements are fulfilled. a. the question raised for the first time in appeal, is a pure question of law and is not a mixed question of law and fact; b. the question raised for the first time in appeal is an issue put forward in the Court below under one of the issues raised; and c. the Court which hears the appeal has before it all the material that is required to decide the question.",SRC030-s150,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1363:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A co-owner can eject a trespasser from the co-owned property without joining the other co-owners as parties to the suit.,even a co-owner has every right to eject the trespassers without making other co-owners parties to the suit.,SRC030-s147,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1364:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A tenant must expressly attorn to the new landlord and continue to pay rent to the transferee from the day of the receipt of the notice of the new transfer, if they want to keep the contract of tenancy.","The tenant has to expressly attorn to the new landlord and continue to pay the rent to the transferee from the day of the receipt of the notice of the new transfer, if he wants to keep the contract of tenancy.",SRC028-s7,ok-unchecked,The Rent Act No. 7 of 1972,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1368:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_8_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A plaintiff must show the grounds on which they seek exemption from the operation of the law of limitation.,"the rule in Section 44 is mandatory and therefore whenever a Plaintiff seeks exemption from the operation of the law of limitation, he must show the grounds on which he seeks such exemption – see Don Palis Appuhamy v. Perera2.",SRC030-s44,section-not-found,Section 44 of the CPC,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1374:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"To establish prescriptive acquisition of immovable property, a defendant must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for more than 10 years.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1375:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is bound to take steps and proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition to prosecute an action without allowing it to accumulate on the case list.,"People may do what they like with their disputes as long as they do not invoke the assistance of the Courts of Law. But whenever that step has been taken they are bound to proceed with all possible and reasonable expedition, and it is the duty of their legal advisors and of the Courts themselves to seek that this is done.",SRC030-s402,section-not-found,Section 402 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1378:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,The progress of prescription is not arrested by the minority of the plaintiffs or the absence of their father beyond the seas.,"the mere fact that his succession passed to the plaintiffs on his death, and that they were minors at the time, cannot arrest the progress of prescription.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 2 of 1889,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1386:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_13_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,Substitution in appellate proceedings does not confer upon the person substituted the status of a legal heir of the deceased party.,"It is therefore not necessary to substitute all heirs of the deceased as parties to the appeal. As I have already stated, substitution is solely for the limited purpose of prosecuting the appeal.",SRC030,ok,Civil Procedure Code No.2 of 1889,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1389:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,Claims arising out of goods sold and delivered become prescribed in 1 year.,"As observed by the learned Judge of the High Court, in terms of section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance, claims arising out of such transactions become prescribed in 1 year.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1391:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A contract for the sale of goods requires a sale and delivery of the goods within a year for the prescriptive period to apply.,"No action shall be maintainable for or in respect of any goods sold and delivered, or for any shop bill or book debt, or for work and labour done, or for the wages of artisans, laborers, or servants, unless the same shall be brought within one year after the debt shall have become due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1392:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,The prescription period for an action without a written agreement is three years from the date of the cause of action.,"In the case of an unwritten agreement, an action should be filed within three years from the breach of the said agreement (from the date of the cause of action), in terms of section 7 of the said Ordinance.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 as amended,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1395:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_2_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof is on the judgment debtor to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,In the light of the foregoing case law this Court finds that the burden of proof is on the Petitioner (judgement – debtor) to establish substantial losses if the writ is issued.,SRC030-s763,ok,section 763 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1397:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action based on a written contract is not prescribed if brought within six years from the date of the breach of such contract.,"Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance provides that; “No action shall be maintainable upon ... any written promise, contract, bargain, or agreement, ... unless such action shall be brought within six years from the date of the breach of such ... contract, bargain or agreement, ... from the date when such note or bill shall have become due, or of the last payment of interest thereon.”",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1399:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_45_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A demand for repayment of a loan repayable on demand will arise only at the time when a demand is made.,"It is settled law that the cause of action concerning a loan repayable on demand will arise only at the time when a demand is made [Seylan Bank Ltd v. Inter Trade Garments (Pvt.) Ltd. (2005) 1 Sri.L.R 80; Sivasubramaniam v. Alagamutui (1950) 53 N.L.R 150; See also, Bank of Ceylon v. Flex Port (Pvt.) Ltd., SC Appeal 120/2012, S.C.M. 03.07.2020 at pages 6-7].",SRC030-s40(d),none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1401:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A defendant who fails to move the court to try an issue as a preliminary issue at the commencement of the trial is deemed to have waived objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of the court to hear and determine the defendant's action.,His failure to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue on such a vital matter will amount to a waiver of objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of Court to hear and determine the defendant’s action.,SRC030-s147,section-not-found,Section 147 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:1405:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed in three years, but in one year if it is based on goods sold and delivered.",An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,Section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
