scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1134:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,"A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights, which must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and must have lasted for at least ten years.","According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1136:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,A person who has been dispossessed of immovable property otherwise than by process of law can institute proceedings against the person dispossessing him at any time within one year of such dispossession.,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance declares: ~,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1138:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof is on the defendant in an action for declaration of title to property where the legal title is in the plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the defendant.,"In an action for declaration of title to property, where the legal title is in the Plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the Defendant, the burden of proof is on the Defendant - See. Siyaneris vs Jayasinghe Udenis de Silva 52 NLR 289(PC) .",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1139:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_561_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_561_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"The trial judge must identify the land to be partitioned first, and if the parties are not in agreement about the identity of the corpus and boundaries, it is the duty of the court to identify the correct land.",If the parties are not in agreement about the identity of the corpus and boundaries it is the duty of court to identify the correct land.,SRC030-s44,section-not-found,section 44 of the Civil Procedure Code,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1146:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,"A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity, and a co-owner's possession in law is tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind unless there is proof of nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
